{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising regimes: when the last 10–20 days show an upswing, the model underpredicts the level by ~+2 to +4 EUR across most of the horizon; apply an upward level shift.",
    "Trend/acceleration under-capture: after a breakout (series moving from low 60s to high 60s), the forecast slope is too flat; increase the drift term for the first ~15–25 days ahead.",
    "Excess mean-reversion: forecasts repeatedly pull back toward mid-60s while realized prices stay elevated (high-60s/near-70); reduce mean-reversion strength when recent realized volatility is accompanied by higher highs.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction grows with horizon in strong uptrends (largest errors often mid-to-late horizon); use a bias ramp (small uplift early, larger uplift later) rather than a constant shift.",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: when the model does project late-horizon spikes (e.g., ~70), it tends to overshoot and/or mistime them; dampen peak amplitudes and shift spike timing earlier if momentum is already present in history.",
    "Level anchoring error: forecasts appear anchored to the recent average (mid-60s) even when the regime has shifted; re-anchor to the last 3–5 day level when the 10-day mean exceeds the 30-day mean by a threshold (e.g., >1 EUR)."
  ]
}
